{
  "description": "#115 Phase 4 (D-115-10) wikilink parity corpus, extended #119 Phase 3 (repair.py retirement). ONE shared dataset pins token semantics across FOUR consumers: the compiler extractor (compiler.validate_source_response.body_wikilink_slugs), the mirrored graph extractor (kdb_graph.intake.body_wikilink_slugs), the canonicalizer rewrite (compiler.canonicalize._remap_body_wikilinks), and the #119 normalization bridge's response-local body policy (compiler.proposal_bridge — the coercion rewrite died with repair.py). Per case THREE expectation surfaces: extracted slugs (parsing), post-canonicalize body (ledger-alias authority), and post-bridge body (response-local authority). `resolve` is the canonicalizer's normalized-surface -> canonical map. `response_pages` (optional) lists the page slugs the case's synthetic response carries: the bridge projection derives its rename via collapse_slug over those slugs ONLY (response-local authority, R6 F2) — without `response_pages` every token is unmapped and `expected_body_bridge` == the body preserved verbatim (the new authority behavior); with it, mapped tokens rewrite occurrence-exact (the authority-valid coverage the retired coercion column carried). The escaped/code-span cases deliberately use MALFORMED targets so a bridge regression that scans protected regions FAILS the corpus (Codex Gate-4 F1).",
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "plain",
      "body": "Alpha links [[aapl]] and [[msft]].",
      "expected_slugs": ["aapl", "msft"],
      "resolve": {"aapl": "apple-inc"},
      "expected_body_canonicalize": "Alpha links [[apple-inc]] and [[msft]].",
      "expected_body_bridge": "Alpha links [[aapl]] and [[msft]]."
    },
    {
      "id": "display-alias",
